## Conversation
Goal: serve moonshotai/Kimi-K3 on arm64 GB300 (sm_103) from self-built images.
The user found flashinfer#4180 and asked to incorporate it; it proved
unbuildable, so support was reconstructed from NVIDIA's public Kimi-K3 image
instead. The matrix shape churned a lot before landing here -- three entries,
then two, then cu12 on the branch, then cu12 reverted, then cu12 dropped
entirely once the team confirmed nobody consumes it. History was rewritten to
hide that churn; this note is the record of it.

## Actions
- Extracted the 31 MB cubin-pool layer and the installed flashinfer package
  from public vllm/vllm-openai:kimi-k3-arm64 via the registry API; diffed that
  package against public v0.6.15.post1 to derive the SiTU patch.
- Iterated a JIT-compile falsification in a GB300 pod until the private module
  compiled, loaded, and exported all six *_private FFI entry points.
- Built and served: correct output at 149.7 tok/s (flashinfer_trtllm TP8 +
  DSpark k=7, AL~3.9). Non-speculative flashinfer 40.3; deep_gemm_mega_moe
  TEP8 48.7, +DSpark 97.2; marlin 7.3 (correct but unusable).

## Errors & Mistakes
- Shipped four images (r1-r5) before noticing pip skipped the patched
  FlashInfer wheels as "already satisfied"; see the preceding commit.
- A rediff of flashinfer#4180 was tried first and CANNOT compile: it asserts
  SiTuGlu into public artifact headers that define only SwiGlu/GeGlu
  ("KernelRunner.h: has no member SiTuGlu"). Do not resurrect it.
- The automatic extraction of the private diff missed three hunks that carry no
  SITU-related token: threading module_name into the final gen_jit_spec call
  (without it the AOT short-circuit loads the PUBLIC module and raises
  AttributeError trtllm_bf16_moe_private), the lean-routing #endif closers
  ("unterminated #else"), and reduceTopKForLane in RoutingKernelTopK.cuh.
  Each was found only by compiling in-cluster.
- FlashInferSetCubinCallback was invisible under -fvisibility=hidden until the
  wheel's cubin_loader.h (visibility("default") macro) was adopted.
- Argued to collapse the build matrix before checking whether the CUDA 12 entry
  had a consumer. It did not, but the order was backwards.

## Dead Ends
- flashinfer#4180 as-is (above).
- Installing NVIDIA's arm64 wheels directly: flashinfer_jit_cache 0.6.15 fails
  the startswith version check against post1 pins and drags ~490 lines of
  unrelated post-0.6.15 drift.
- K3 on CUDA 12: vLLM gates fused_kda_decode and kimi_k3_attn_res on
  CUDA >= 13.0 with 10.0f/12.0f arch specs 12.9 cannot emit. Measured failure:
  "_OpNamespace '_C' object has no attribute 'kimi_k3_attn_res'". nvcc 12.9
  DOES target sm_103a -- that was not the blocker.
- marlin and triton as fast backends: marlin is correct but 7.3 tok/s; the
  MXFP4 wrapper rejects triton for SITU outright.

## Hints for Future Agents
- The pool is an OVERLAY, not a replacement: zero SwiGLU fused-act kernels, so
  never repoint ArtifactPath at it.
- The private module JIT-compiles on the first MoE call, ~4 min per node.
  Prewarming it into the image is the obvious follow-up and was not done.
- vLLM's kimi-k3 branch passes is_private=True unconditionally in
  trtllm_{mxfp4,nvfp4}_moe.py, so this API is a hard requirement of the branch,
  not an optimisation.
- MAX_JOBS defaults to 32; the FlashInfer AOT stage takes ~6 h at that on a
  128-CPU builder and 73 min at MAX_JOBS=128. sccache-as-ccbin does not cache
  nvcc device compiles, so this stage never speeds up from the S3 cache.
- Serving needs same-NVLink-domain placement (label
  ds.coreweave.com/nvlink.domain): both of K3's torch symmetric-memory call
  sites fail across racks. See the infr repo's commit notes for the full
  serving contract.

## Codebase Discoveries
- vllm/utils/deep_gemm.py prefers site-packages `deep_gemm` over the vendored
  vllm.third_party copy (its docstring says so), which is why the wheel rev had
  to match cmake's vendored pin.
- The v0.6.14 deepseekv3-no-group-routing patch was upstreamed in
  v0.6.15.post1: mSumEpsilon plus the applyWithAux/PolicyTraits specialisation.

